.\" Manual page for fm84. View with: man ./doc/fm84.1
.TH FM84 1 "2026-10-13" "fm84 0.26.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fm84 \- a synthwave dual\-pane TUI file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fm84
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ LEFT
.RI [ RIGHT ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fm84
is a two\-panel file manager for the terminal, in the line of Norton
Commander and Midnight Commander.
Each panel lists a directory, with tabs of its own; files are copied and
moved from the active panel to the other one.
It has a built\-in viewer with syntax highlighting, hex and image views, a
built\-in editor, find files by name and content, and directory sizes counted
in the background.
.PP
Anything that touches the disk is done off the thread that draws the screen,
so a network mount that has stopped answering never freezes the interface;
Esc stops waiting on it.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I LEFT
Directory for the left panel.
.TP
.I RIGHT
Directory for the right panel.
.PP
A directory given takes the place of the tab that would have shown; the
panel's other tabs stay.
Without one, a panel opens where it was when fm84 last quit if
.B Remember directories
is on (F11), and in the current directory otherwise.
A directory that does not exist is reported before anything starts.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help, with where the config file is, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End the options, for a directory whose name starts with a dash.
.SH KEYS
F1 inside fm84 lists these too.
.SS Panels
.TP 18
.B Up Down Left Right
Move the cursor.
.TP
.B Tab
Switch panels.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the directory, or run the file.
.TP
.B Backspace
Go to the parent directory.
.TP
.B Home End
First or last entry.
.TP
.B PageUp PageDown
A page up or down.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Left Ctrl+Right
Open the directory under the cursor in the left or right panel.
.TP
.I letters and digits
Quick search: jump to the first name starting so, either case; Up and Down
go between the names that do.
.TP
.B Esc
Clear the search; close a dialog, the viewer or the editor; stop counting
directory sizes; stop waiting on a directory that has not answered.
.TP
.B Space Insert
Select or deselect the entry.
Space on a directory also counts its size.
.TP
.B + \-
Select or deselect by pattern:
.B *
any run of characters,
.B ?
any one,
.B [abc]
and
.B [!a\-z]
classes, several separated by
.BR ; .
A pattern ending in
.B /
is for directories.
All in lower case matches either case.
.TP
.B *
Invert the selection of files.
.TP
.B Alt+*
Invert the selection of files and directories.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Reload both panels.
.TP
.B Ctrl+T Ctrl+W
New tab, close tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+PgUp Ctrl+PgDn
Previous or next tab.
.TP
.B Alt+1 ... Alt+9
Go to tab 1 to 8, or the last.
.TP
.B Alt+F1 Alt+F2
Choose a drive for the left or right panel.
Ctrl works too.
.SS Function keys
.TP 18
.B F1
Help.
.TP
.B F2
Rename.
.TP
.B F3
View the file.
.TP
.B F4
Edit the file, in the built\-in editor or the one set under F11.
.TP
.B F5
Copy to the other panel, or wherever the dialog is told.
A single file can be given a new name on the way.
.TP
.B F6
Move, as F5 copies.
.TP
.B F7
Create a directory.
.TP
.B Shift+F4
Create an empty file.
.TP
.B F8 Delete
Move to the trash, or delete if F11 says so.
.TP
.B Shift+F8 Shift+Delete
Delete for good.
.TP
.B F9
Open a terminal in the panel's directory.
.TP
.B F10
Quit.
Twice while a copy, move or delete runs; asks first about unsaved edits.
.TP
.B F11
Options.
.TP
.B F12
Preview the file under the cursor in the other panel.
.TP
.B Alt+F7 Ctrl+F7
Find files by name and, if asked, by content, down from the active panel's
directory.
.SS During a copy, move or delete
.TP 18
.B Esc
Cancel it.
.TP
.B R S A
Retry, Skip, or Skip all, when an entry fails.
.TP
.B Y N A S O
Overwrite, Skip, Overwrite all, Skip all, or Overwrite all older, when a
file is already there.
.TP
.B D
Delete for good, when the trash will not take an entry.
.SS Viewer
.TP 18
.B X
Hex view on or off; for an image, cycle Image, Text and Hex.
.TP
.B W
Wrap long lines, or scroll them sideways.
.TP
.B F
Fit or fill an image.
.TP
.B + \-
Zoom an image in or out.
.TP
.B PageUp PageDown
Previous or next picture in the folder, when one is open.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Copy the selection.
.SS Editor
.TP 18
.B Shift+arrows
Select text.
.TP
.B Ctrl+A
Select all.
.TP
.B Ctrl+X Ctrl+C Ctrl+V
Cut, copy, paste.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z
Undo.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Y Ctrl+Shift+Z
Redo.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R F4
Replace; then
.BR Y ,
.B N
or
.B A
to replace, skip or replace all.
.TP
.B O R
Overwrite or reload, when the file changed on disk since it was opened.
.SS Viewer and editor
.TP 18
.B Ctrl+F
Find.
All in lower case matches either case.
.TP
.B F3 Shift+F3
Next or previous match.
.TP
.B Ctrl+G
Go to line.
.SH OPTIONS DIALOG
F11 opens the options, changed in place with Enter, Space, Left or Right and
saved as they change.
Among them: hidden files, sort order, columns, date format and size units;
confirmations, delete to trash, what to do when a destination exists, the
terminal and editor commands, remembering directories; the theme, icons and
clock; tab width, line numbers, highlighting and image settings.
.PP
In the
.B Terminal
and
.B Editor
commands,
.B {}
stands for the directory or the file.
An editor without
.B {}
gets the file last.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/config
The options, as plain
.I key = value
lines, fine to edit by hand.
.I ~/.config/fm84/config
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not set, and
.I %APPDATA%\efm84\econfig
on Windows.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/session
The panels' directories and tabs, kept when
.B Remember directories
is on.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the config directory is; see
.BR FILES .
.TP
.B HOME
The config directory when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not set, and where a typed
.B ~
leads.
.TP
.B TERMINAL
The terminal F9 opens when none is set under F11.
Without either, fm84 tries
.BR xdg\-terminal\-emulator ,
.BR alacritty ,
.BR kitty ,
.BR foot ,
.BR gnome\-terminal ,
.BR konsole ,
.B xfce4\-terminal
and
.BR xterm ,
in that order.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the trash is, as the freedesktop.org trash specification has it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Quit normally.
.TP
.B 1
Not run from a terminal, or an error that stopped it.
.TP
.B 2
A mistake on the command line, such as an unknown option or a directory
that does not exist.
.SH NOTES
Icons need a Nerd Font; F11 can switch them to plain letters.
Images are drawn as ASCII art in colour, and look best in a terminal with
true colour.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR mv (1)
.PP
https://github.com/coltwillcox/fm84
.SH AUTHOR
Written by coltwillcox.
